# 4. Document and Page Architecture

This is one of the most important features of One Book.

There should be three fundamentally different page formats.

Users should be able to switch between them through document settings.

## Format 1: Split Pages

This is the conventional notebook experience.

Each page should be a separate A4 sheet with a visible gap between consecutive sheets.

Requirements:

- Each page must be exactly A4.
- Support portrait and landscape orientation.
- Each sheet should have clearly defined boundaries.
- Page colour and template should be independently configurable at document level.
- Pages should appear automatically as the user continues writing or scrolling.
- Users should never have to manually click an Add Page button to continue writing.
- Content should be clipped to its respective page boundaries.
- Support both vertical and horizontal scrolling.

When vertical scrolling is selected, pages should be stacked vertically.

When horizontal scrolling is selected, pages should be arranged horizontally.

The space between pages should be subtle and consistent.

## Format 2: Infinite Scroll

This should behave like a continuous notebook made of A4 pages.

Unlike Split Pages, consecutive sheets should have no visible gap between them.

Requirements:

- A4 page dimensions remain fixed.
- Support portrait and landscape orientation.
- Pages should be seamlessly connected.
- No unnecessary margins between consecutive pages.
- New pages should appear automatically as the user approaches the end of the existing writing area.
- No manual page creation should be necessary.
- Support vertical and horizontal scrolling.
- Templates and page colours should continue consistently between pages.

For example, in vertical portrait mode, the notebook should behave like an endless vertical strip of connected A4 sheets.

In horizontal mode, the same principle should apply horizontally.

## Format 3: Infinite Canvas

This should provide a genuinely unrestricted workspace.

There should be no fixed page boundaries or predetermined canvas dimensions.

Requirements:

- Users should be able to pan indefinitely.
- Users should be able to zoom in and out.
- The visible workspace should expand dynamically as the user pans or zooms into new areas.
- Drawing and writing should remain anchored to their original world coordinates.
- No visible page transitions or gaps.
- Templates should repeat continuously across the entire workspace.
- The background should remain consistent regardless of how far the user travels across the canvas.

The application must not create one enormous bitmap for the infinite background.

Instead, use a repeating pattern or dynamically rendered background tiles.

As the viewport moves, render only the tiles currently required.

### CRITICAL: Switching Between Formats

Implement this carefully.

The three formats must share a common underlying document-content model.

Changing the format must NEVER delete existing content.

For example:

1. A user writes something on the right side of an infinite canvas.
2. They switch to Split Pages.
3. Content outside the active A4 page regions should disappear from view.
4. The content must remain saved internally.
5. Switching back to Infinite Canvas must restore the original content in exactly its previous location.

The same principle applies to Infinite Scroll.

Use clipping regions and presentation-level visibility rules.

Do not destructively resize, reposition, crop or delete content when switching formats.

Think of page formats as different ways of presenting the same underlying workspace.

