# 13. Core TypeScript Data Models

Create a strongly typed document model.

Use a unified underlying content model across all document formats.

A suggested starting point:

```typescript
type DocumentMode =
  | "split-pages"
  | "infinite-scroll"
  | "infinite-canvas";

type PageOrientation =
  | "portrait"
  | "landscape";

type ScrollDirection =
  | "vertical"
  | "horizontal";

type PageSize = "A4";

type TemplateType =
  | "lined"
  | "grid"
  | "dot"
  | "oxford"
  | "custom";

interface PageSettings {
  size: PageSize;
  orientation: PageOrientation;
  backgroundColor: string;
  template: TemplateConfig;
}

interface DocumentSettings {
  mode: DocumentMode;
  scrollDirection: ScrollDirection;
  page: PageSettings;
}

interface TemplateConfig {
  type: TemplateType;
  variation?: "standard" | "narrow" | "wide";
  assetId?: string;
}

interface Point {
  x: number;
  y: number;
  pressure: number;
  timestamp: number;
}

interface InkStroke {
  id: string;
  points: Point[];
  color: string;
  width: number;
  tool: "fountain" | "ballpoint" | "marker"
      | "pencil" | "brush" | "highlighter";
}

interface NotebookDocument {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  settings: DocumentSettings;
  content: DocumentContent;
  createdAt: number;
  updatedAt: number;
}
```

Expand this model with appropriate types for:

- Shapes.
- Imported images.
- Custom template assets.
- Text content.
- Page positions.
- Selection state.
- Canvas viewport.
- Document history.

Store drawing coordinates independently of screen coordinates.

Use a consistent document/world coordinate system so that changing zoom, orientation or viewport does not corrupt existing content.

